# Story Loom project rules

- Before editing, read AGENTS.md, PROJECT_RULES.md, and the code and tests
  relevant to the task. Check the working tree and preserve unrelated work.
  Use the current checkout as the baseline.
- Extend the existing Next.js, TypeScript, Prisma, and SQLite app in place.
  Reuse existing services and UI patterns. Story Loom is the display name.
  Preserve legacy routes, config keys, and identifiers that callers depend on.
- Preserve record IDs, exact prose, unknown fields, revisions, provenance,
  protection, canon status, scene parents and order, links, images, Trash,
  and applied-package history.
- Keep supported Wordly backups and .wordly.json packages readable. Preserve
  stale-revision checks, protection, atomic apply, and idempotency. Version new
  formats when needed and reject unsupported versions before writing.
- Verify migrations and restores against isolated databases. Use invented
  fixtures in the repo and private restored copies for real manuscript data.
  Back up the working database and verify restore before migrating it.
  Never reset or reseed the working database.
- Implement the requested milestone. Run the relevant checks and report
  actual results, compatibility limits, and rollback steps. Update Help and
  PROGRESS.md when behavior changes. Push or deploy only when requested.
- Use one project header. `LandingHeader` (or `ProjectPageHeader`, which loads
  the cover and series line) is the header on every project page. Keep the
  cover and eyebrow. Change only `title`. On a nested page, pass `breadcrumbs`
  or `crumbs`. Do not add a second page title with a bare `h1` or `page-head`.